    <div class="block">
      <div class="block-title"><div class="badge red">FE</div><h3>Frontend basics — light coverage for the reported HTML/CSS/JS task</h3></div>
      <div class="block-meta">Not a deep-dive — just enough to not lose free points if this question type appears. 30–45 minutes total, not a full day.</div>
      <ul class="checklist" data-group="day2">
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d2-26"></span><label for="d2-26">Core HTML tags, form inputs, basic semantic structure</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d2-27"></span><label for="d2-27">CSS selectors, box model, flexbox basics (enough to fix layout, not design it)</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d2-28"></span><label for="d2-28">Vanilla JS DOM basics: <code>querySelector</code>, event listeners, updating text/attributes on click</label></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note">Reported as an editing/fill-in style task on an existing page rather than "build a UI from scratch" — so recognizing and modifying DOM code matters more than design skill. If your slot doesn't include this, this block cost you under an hour either way.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="block">
      <div class="block-title"><div class="badge amber">TS</div><h3>Coding-round time strategy — rehearse this exact flow</h3></div>
      <div class="timeline-legend">
        <span><span class="dot" style="background:var(--ink)"></span>Read &amp; classify</span>
        <span><span class="dot" style="background:var(--teal)"></span>Easiest problem</span>
        <span><span class="dot" style="background:var(--amber)"></span>Second problem</span>
        <span><span class="dot" style="background:var(--red)"></span>Third + debug</span>
      </div>
      <div class="timeline-bar">
        <div class="timeline-seg" style="width:8.3%; background:var(--ink);">5</div>
        <div class="timeline-seg" style="width:33.3%; background:var(--teal);">15–20</div>
        <div class="timeline-seg" style="width:33.3%; background:var(--amber);">20</div>
        <div class="timeline-seg" style="width:25%; background:var(--red);">remaining</div>
      </div>
      <div class="time-strategy">
        <div class="row"><span class="t">First 5 min</span><span>Read all 3 problems, classify each (array/string? hashing? two-pointer? sort/search? harder?)</span></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="t">Next 15–20 min</span><span>Solve the easiest one completely, including edge cases</span></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="t">Next 20 min</span><span>Solve the second problem</span></div>
        <div class="row"><span class="t">Remaining time</span><span>Attempt the third + debug + run through test cases on all three</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note"><strong>Don't fight one problem for 35 minutes.</strong> If a question isn't clicking after ~15–20 minutes, bank partial credit and move on.</div>
      <div class="note">Candidate reports commonly cite the clearance bar as roughly <strong>2 fully-passing solutions + 1 partial</strong> (not all 3 perfect) — so a clean, fully-tested solution on two beats a rushed, broken attempt at all three. If the mix really is DSA + SQL + Frontend, your SQL query is usually the fastest of the three to get fully correct — consider doing it first to bank a guaranteed pass, then spend remaining time on the DSA problem, with frontend last since it's the most likely to be skippable if time runs out.</div>
      <p style="font-size:13.5px; margin:14px 0 0;">Today, practice at least one <strong>timed 3-question / 60-minute set</strong> — not just isolated untimed problems.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ROUND 3 -->
  <section class="panel" id="day3">
    <div class="day-head"><h2>Round 3 — Behavioral + Cognitive</h2><span class="day-date">40 min · personality + gamified puzzles, only reached after clearing Round 2</span></div>
    <p class="day-objective">Personality assessment plus immersive/gamified puzzles and numerical equations. This round genuinely needs the least prep time of the four — don't over-invest here.</p>
    <div class="progress-track"><div class="progress-fill" id="prog-day3"></div></div>
    <div class="tri-legend">
      <span class="lg"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--red)"></span>Fix now</span>
      <span class="lg"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--amber)"></span>Needs practice</span>
      <span class="lg"><span class="dot" style="background:var(--teal)"></span>Ready</span>
      <span class="hint">Tap any item to cycle its confidence</span>
    </div>

    <div class="block">
      <div class="block-title"><div class="badge amber">CG</div><h3>Cognitive — puzzles + numerical equations</h3></div>
      <div class="block-meta">Researched from 2025–26 candidate reports — task names vary by drive, but the underlying skills are consistent. Verify what actually loads on your mock.</div>

      <div class="subgroup-label">Numerical</div>
      <ul class="checklist" data-group="day3">
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-9"></span><label for="d4-9">Percentages, ratios, averages</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-10"></span><label for="d4-10">Number sequences, basic equations</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-11"></span><label for="d4-11">Profit/loss, speed/distance/time basics</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-24"></span><label for="d4-24">Rapid single/two-digit arithmetic under a tight timer — candidates report this is the one task with quant overlap, but far faster-paced than a normal aptitude section</label></li>
      </ul>

      <div class="subgroup-label">Commonly reported gamified task types</div>
      <ul class="checklist" data-group="day3">
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-12"></span><label for="d4-12"><b>Path/route puzzle</b> — trace the shortest route through a grid around obstacles, against a per-puzzle timer. Widely reported as the most heavily weighted task.</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-13"></span><label for="d4-13"><b>Sequence/pattern recall</b> — a sequence of shapes or colours flashes, then you replay it in order; length grows each round (Simon-Says style)</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-25"></span><label for="d4-25"><b>Logic-move puzzle</b> — a Tower-of-Hanoi-style disk puzzle, moving pieces to a target arrangement in the fewest moves</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-26"></span><label for="d4-26"><b>Reaction-time task</b> — press the correct key on a prompt while ignoring decoy prompts, scored on speed + accuracy together</label></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="subgroup-label">Practice sources (free, no prep needed)</div>
      <ul class="checklist" data-group="day3">
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-14"></span><label for="d4-14">A "shortest path" grid puzzle app or Lumosity-style path game, timed</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-27"></span><label for="d4-27">Simon-Says / color-sequence memory app</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-28"></span><label for="d4-28">A Tower of Hanoi solver/practice app — get the recursive move pattern into muscle memory</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-29"></span><label for="d4-29">Human Benchmark's reaction-time test (free, browser-based) for a few rounds</label></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note">Reported coverage is uneven — many drives still run the older verbal/numerical/logical MCQ format instead of games, and exact task names differ across sources. Don't over-prepare one task: reports suggest balanced-but-average performance across tasks clears the cutoff better than being excellent at one and weak at another. Treat the list above as <strong>practice direction</strong>, not a confirmed spec — your own mock assessment is the real answer for what you'll see.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="block">
      <div class="block-title"><div class="badge red">BH</div><h3>Behavioral (personality) — 30 minutes or less, genuinely</h3></div>
      <ul class="checklist" data-group="day3">
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-15"></span><label for="d4-15">Skim workplace-behavior themes: teamwork, accountability, adaptability, professionalism</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-16"></span><label for="d4-16">Decide your honest answering approach in advance</label></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note">Personality assessments flag inconsistency, not any single "wrong" answer. <strong>Consistency &gt; optimization</strong> here. There's no productive multi-hour curriculum for this section — half an hour (or less) of skimming is genuinely enough; don't let it eat into Day 4's technical time.</div>
    </div>

  </section>

