    <div class="block">
      <div class="block-title"><div class="badge">CM</div><h3>Communication — the real, automated test format</h3></div>
      <div class="block-meta">~25–30 min, software-graded (not a human on the call). Candidate reports converge on a consistent section list, though exact section count/order varies by drive.</div>

      <div class="subgroup-label">Reported sections, in typical order</div>
      <ul class="checklist" data-group="day4">
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-17"></span><label for="d4-17"><b>Reading</b> — a passage, then MCQs on it; read the questions first, then skim for answers</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-33"></span><label for="d4-33"><b>Listening</b> — an audio clip, then questions on main idea, detail, or speaker's attitude</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-34"></span><label for="d4-34"><b>Sentence repetition</b> — hear a sentence, repeat it aloud exactly, clearly, at a steady pace</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-35"></span><label for="d4-35"><b>Jumbled-sentence reordering</b> — a sentence is read back in a scrambled word order; you say it back in the correct, natural order</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-36"></span><label for="d4-36"><b>Rapid spoken Q&amp;A</b> — a run of short, simple, everyday questions, one spoken answer each — this is scored on fluency/vocabulary, not "correctness"</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-37"></span><label for="d4-37"><b>Open-ended speaking</b> — describe/explain something for 45–60 seconds using a point → reason → example → conclusion structure</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-38"></span><label for="d4-38">Some drives add a short <b>email-writing</b> task at the end if you pass the spoken sections — professional tone, clear ask, proper close</label></li>
      </ul>

      <div class="subgroup-label">Grammar priority (feeds both spoken and written scoring)</div>
      <ul class="checklist" data-group="day4">
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-20"></span><label for="d4-20">Subject-verb agreement, tenses, articles</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-21"></span><label for="d4-21">Prepositions, conditionals, active/passive voice</label></li>
      </ul>

      <div class="subgroup-label">Written etiquette</div>
      <ul class="checklist" data-group="day4">
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-22"></span><label for="d4-22">Professional etiquette — greeting, clear request, professional close</label></li>
        <li><span class="tri" data-id="d4-23"></span><label for="d4-23">Write one short professional email; proofread it</label></li>
      </ul>

      <div class="note">Because it's software-graded: start speaking the instant the beep/prompt appears (a slow start eats your response window), speak at a steady ~80% pace rather than rushing and slurring, and test your headset/mic in a quiet room beforehand. Multiple reports note technically strong candidates get filtered out here purely from setup issues or nerves, not language ability — do at least one full practice run out loud before your real slot.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

